WEBVTT

NOTE Illustrative fixture (PRD Appendix A.1)

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.000
<v Interviewer>[M2.2] Walk me through the last flight you planned, step by step, as it actually went.</v>

00:06:40.000 --> 00:07:55.000
<v Priya Nair>First thing, I build the preliminary load plan off the booked figures, usually around T-120. Takes me about twenty minutes.</v>

00:07:55.000 --> 00:14:05.000
<v Priya Nair>For widebodies, honestly only me and one other controller really know the configurations.</v>

00:14:05.000 --> 00:16:22.000
<v Priya Nair>Then I check special loads, dangerous goods and live animals, against the checklist. The notification from the cargo terminal often comes late.</v>

00:16:22.000 --> 00:21:45.000
<v Priya Nair>If something can't be accepted, the airline decides what to offload. That takes anywhere from fifteen to forty minutes. Then I re-plan.</v>

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:50.000
<v Interviewer>How long did each part take, and where were you waiting on someone or something?</v>

00:21:50.000 --> 00:22:30.000
<v Priya Nair>Ramp loads the aircraft, forty minutes or so, and sends back the loading report. It's still on paper and I re-key it. We sometimes wait fifteen minutes for it, and at peak we're short of containers.</v>

00:22:30.000 --> 00:24:10.000
<v Priya Nair>Officially the ramp should send the report within ten minutes. What actually happens on a busy wave is I walk down and read it off their sheet, otherwise we'd miss the slot.</v>

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:40.000
<v Priya Nair>My rule of thumb: if the forward hold looks heavy on the plan, I call the ramp before they start, not after. Nobody taught me that; I learned it after we had to re-trim three flights in one night.</v>

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:46.000
<v Interviewer>[M5.s1] Was that night more like following the procedure, needing an expert, working it out as you went, or firefighting?</v>

00:24:46.000 --> 00:27:15.000
<v Priya Nair>Firefighting. It happens maybe once a month.</v>

00:27:15.000 --> 00:29:02.000
<v Priya Nair>If the weight deviation is outside tolerance, it's the captain who decides, not us. The tolerance depends on the airline contract.</v>

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:40.000
<v Priya Nair>The captain either accepts or asks for a re-trim. Five to fifteen minutes.</v>

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:48.000
<v Interviewer>[M4.3] How do you decide what to propose when it's outside tolerance? Is there a rule for this, or does it depend?</v>

00:29:48.000 --> 00:31:10.000
<v Priya Nair>There's no fixed answer. It depends on the captain, the weather, what's still on the belt. You read the situation.</v>

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:15.000
<v Priya Nair>Then I issue the final load sheet, around T-15.</v>
